  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2014 scholarly source collection on Prussian military socialization has generated steady reader engagement, with nearly 2,000 downloads recorded through the OAPEN platform. The work circulates as an open-access hybrid volume within an established German archive series, reaching researchers through library discovery systems and digital repositories.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Milit\u00e4r und Gesellschaft in Preu\u00dfen \u2013 Quellen zur Milit\u00e4rsozialisation 1713\u20131806, edited by J\u00fcrgen Kloosterhuis, Bernhard R. Kroener, Klaus Neitmann, and Ralf Pr\u00f6ve, functions as a curated source collection bridging archival documentation and historical research. Published by Peter Lang in 2014, it forms volume 27 of the \"Quellen, Findbuecher und Inventare des Brandenburgischen Landeshauptarchivs\" series, positioning it within a sustained editorial program of German regional archive guides and inventories that continues to the present day.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The most visible measure of uptake is reader download activity: the work has accumulated 1,986 downloads via the OAPEN open-access library, with consistent monthly usage ranging from 143 to 268 downloads across available months. This pattern indicates active consultation by students, researchers, and archivists rather than a single spike, suggesting the volume serves a recurring research need in military history and German institutional studies.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work is discoverable through multiple institutional routes. WorldCat records document holdings in libraries catalogued under military history, Prussian history, and German archives. The volume is also indexed in the Directory of Open Access Books (DOAB), enhancing its reach within the scholarly open-access ecosystem. Its peer-review credentials are affirmed through inclusion in Peter Lang International Academic Publishers' peer-reviewed series apparatus.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation uptake in OpenAlex does not yet register for this work, though the absence of recorded citations does not preclude its use as a research resource. For source editions and archival guides, discoverability and download metrics often provide more meaningful evidence of scholarly utility than formal citation counts, particularly in German and European history where source collections anchor methodological practice without always appearing as explicit citations in subsequent publications.</p>",
